<BaseLayout
  title="About — SR Soft LLC"
  description="About SR Soft LLC — two decades of enterprise SAP and AI consulting."
  current="about"
>
<!-- ============================================================
     EDITORIAL ABOUT HEADER
     Full-width composition with inline meta strip
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="about-v2-head" data-screen-label="01 About">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-v2-head-grid">
      <div class="about-v2-head-main">
        <div class="editorial-rule">
          <span>About SR Soft</span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="about-v2-headline">Built by SAP consultants<br>now bringing <em>AI</em><br>to the systems they ran.</h1>
        <p class="about-v2-lead">Two decades of enterprise SAP and ERP work — implementations, migrations, integrations, support — extended into the AI era. Headquartered in Sugar Land, Texas. Engagements across the United States.</p>

        <table class="about-v2-fact-table" aria-label="SR Soft at a glance">
          <caption><span class="fact-table-eyebrow">At a glance</span></caption>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Founded</th>
              <td>2004</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Headquarters</th>
              <td>Sugar Land, Texas</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Heritage</th>
              <td>SAP since 2004 · AI since 2022</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Coverage</th>
              <td>Engagements across the United States</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Practices</th>
              <td>SAP, AI, Data, Pharma, Cloud, Management</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Work authorization</th>
              <td>E-Verify, F-1 OPT, STEM OPT, H-1B</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <figure class="head-figure head-figure-logo">
        <div class="head-figure-logo-inner">
          <img src="/logo-mark.png" alt="SR Soft" class="brand-card-mark head-logo-mark">
          <p class="head-logo-name">SR Soft <em>LLC</em></p>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     THE STORY — Long-form prose with drop cap
     The real "about" narrative the original page was missing.
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="about-v2-story" data-screen-label="02 Story">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="about-v2-story-grid">
      <aside class="about-v2-story-side reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">The story</p>
        <h2 class="display-3">Two decades <em>of work,</em><br>not a pitch deck.</h2>
        <div class="about-v2-story-stamp">
          <span class="stamp-line">Volume</span>
          <span class="stamp-num">I.</span>
          <span class="stamp-line">2004 — present</span>
        </div>
      </aside>

      <div class="about-v2-story-prose reveal">
        <p>SR Soft was started in 2004 by a small group of SAP consultants who had spent the dot-com years inside Fortune 500 IT — running R/3 implementations, untangling integrations, sitting through audits, and watching too many strategy decks land softly on conference tables. The work that succeeded had a different shape than the work that didn't. After enough engagements, the pattern was unmissable.</p>

        <p>What persisted survived contact with reality. Production systems. Real users. Auditable trails. The work that failed had usually skipped one of those — pilots that never integrated, dashboards no one opened, reports without the underlying data to be useful. The successful work looked unglamorous up close. It usually was.</p>

        <p>For the first fifteen years the firm's center of gravity was SAP — ECC, then S/4HANA, brownfield migrations, BW and HANA modeling, integrations across CRM and HCM, ABAP development, Basis support. Tens of thousands of consultant-hours inside production systems for clients in pharma, energy, financial services, and consumer goods.</p>

        <p>The shift toward AI began around 2022. Not as a pivot — as an extension. The same SAP systems we'd implemented, the same data we'd modeled, the same business processes we'd documented now needed AI on top of them. SAP BTP, ABAP Cloud, Joule — and the agent and RAG systems built around them — became the next chapter of work that started in 2004.</p>

        <p>Today SR Soft is headquartered in Sugar Land, Texas, with consultants placed on engagements across the United States — most of them onsite or hybrid with Fortune 500 clients. We hire continuously, sponsor F-1 OPT and STEM OPT extensions, and participate in E-Verify. The work has shifted toward AI in the last few years because the work always does. The operating philosophy — <em>strategy beats stack, restraint is a feature, and the goal is to make ourselves unnecessary</em> — hasn't changed in twenty years.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================
     SIX PRINCIPLES — Editorial numbered list (mirroring practices)
     ============================================================ -->
<section class="section principles-v2" data-screen-label="03 Principles">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="section-head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">How we work</p>
      <h2 class="display-3">Six principles. <em>Non-negotiable.</em></h2>
      <p>Each principle is a constraint we put on ourselves. They limit which projects we take, how we staff them, and what we hand off at the end. They aren't aspirational — they're how engagements actually run.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="principles-v2-list reveal">
      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="num">01</span>
        <div class="principle-body">
          <h3>Strategy <em>beats stack.</em></h3>
          <p>The right model on the wrong process produces nothing useful. We start with the workflow, not the technology — every engagement opens with the question of what work needs to change, before any conversation about what to buy or build.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="num">02</span>
        <div class="principle-body">
          <h3>Human-in-the-loop is <em>a design choice.</em></h3>
          <p>We design for it explicitly. AI that recommends, humans that decide — at least until the data is overwhelming that we should change that. The default is augmentation, not autonomy.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="num">03</span>
        <div class="principle-body">
          <h3>Your data is <em>yours.</em></h3>
          <p>Built on your infrastructure. Your cloud, your storage, your control. No black boxes. No proprietary platforms you can't leave. Vendor lock-in is a tax we don't impose on clients.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="num">04</span>
        <div class="principle-body">
          <h3>If we can't explain it, <em>we won't ship it.</em></h3>
          <p>Every system we deliver, we can defend. Architecture, decisions, tradeoffs — written down, walked through, agreed to before code gets written. Production systems with no documented "why" don't leave our engagements.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="num">05</span>
        <div class="principle-body">
          <h3>The goal is to make ourselves <em>unnecessary.</em></h3>
          <p>Engagements end with documentation, training, and a team that can extend the work without us. Retainers are for new problems, not maintenance debt. We win when you don't need us anymore.</p>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="principle-row">
        <span class="num">06</span>
        <div class="principle-body">
          <h3>Restraint is <em>a feature.</em></h3>
          <p>The best AI deployment is often the smallest one. We say no to projects that won't survive contact with reality, even when the budget is approved. That's the most expensive thing we do — and it's also why clients come back.</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

